  "notes": "Championship-turntablist-turned-producer making sparse, pitched, high-gloss R&B-pop and future bass. Both modes the entry names are half-time — \"the trap-R&B mode is half-time and the future-bass mode sits at 140—150 with the backbeat on 3\" — so `halfTime` is true at `halftime_3` with the mode at the stated future-bass centre. The kit is the turntablist one: \"turntablist precision — tiny, dry, transient-forward kicks and snaps, half-time backbeat, and scratch/edit artefacts used as fills; hat patterns are sparse 16ths or absent.\" So the velocity tiers are quiet and tight, the snare is `hard` transient under a `short` tail with a `snap`-only stack, `hihat.continuous` is false at `fillDensity` 0.08, and the fills are `reverse` and `perc2` lanes rather than a roll ladder, over the hardest quantize in this batch — DMC-level editing precision is a grid, not a feel. The low end is the entry's most unusual claim and it is authored as written: \"the bass is usually a pitched chord stack rather than a single note; where an 808 is present it is short and dry. Low-end movement follows the chord, not the kick.\" So `bass808` is `counter_riff` with a `staccato` sustain, a near-zero slide and no distortion, while the bassline is `independent_riff` at a high `followRootsProb` (it follows the chord) with the highest `glideProb` in this section, because the future-bass idiom means \"detuned chord stacks played as a bass instrument, with pitch-bend and vibrato automation on the chord itself\". Harmony is \"minor and modal with maj7/add9 colour\" over 2- or 4-bar loops, which is the low `triad` share and the short `chordDurationBeats`. \"Pitched, granular vocal chops as the lead instrument, plus bells, marimba and plucked synths\" is the melody timbre and the `bell_echo` counter. ⚠ `confidence` is medium against a medium-high rating because the research says the harmonic detail is \"future-bass-conventional\": every drum probability, the velocity tiers and the humanize pair are INFERRED.",
